# Narrative for the running example (numbers below come from actually running running_code.py).
INTRO = {
 1: "Meet the dataset we'll come back to in every lesson: 12 homes for sale in Chennai (made up, but realistic). Each row is one house — an <strong>example</strong>. The columns are <strong>features</strong> (area, size, bedrooms, distance to the centre, age, the listing text) and two possible <strong>labels</strong>: the price in lakh rupees (a regression target) and whether it sold within 30 days (a classification target).",
 2: "Each house becomes a vector of numbers, and the whole table becomes a matrix $X$ with 12 rows and 4 columns. Houses 1 and 2 are 26 units apart (mostly because their sizes differ by 25 m²). Multiplying $X$ by a weight vector gives a prediction for every house at once.",
 3: "Take the simplest model, $\\text{price} = w\\times\\text{size}$. The derivative of the loss tells us which way to move $w$: at $w = 0.4$ the slope is strongly negative (increase $w$), at $w = 0.9$ it's strongly positive (decrease $w$), and near $w = 0.65$ it's almost flat — close to the best value (about 0.6545).",
 4: "The average price is 72.1 lakh with a standard deviation of 17.3, so house 12 (96 lakh) has a z-score of 1.38. Half of all houses sold fast, but 75% of Central ones did: $P(\\text{fast} \\mid \\text{Central}) = 0.75$. Turned around, only half of the fast sellers were Central — remember $P(A\\mid B) \\ne P(B\\mid A)$.",
 5: "With only 12 houses, a single split leaves just 3 for testing — far too few to trust. Cross-validation uses every house for testing once: predicting price from size alone, the error per fold ranges from 8.9 to 20 lakh. That spread is a warning that such a small dataset gives noisy estimates.",
 6: "Size alone explains little ($R^2 = 0.19$): big houses far from the centre are cheap. Adding distance and age lifts $R^2$ to 0.91. Read the weights: each extra m² adds about 0.72 lakh, each km from the centre subtracts about 2 lakh, and each year of age about 1 lakh — holding the others fixed.",
 7: "Gradient descent on standardised size reaches the same line as the formula in Lesson 6 — slope 0.29 and intercept 41.11 — after converting back to m². Two methods, one answer.",
 8: "Can we predict which houses sell fast? Both weights are negative (on standardised features): farther and older houses are less likely to sell fast. A new 5-year-old house 3 km from the centre gets an 88% chance; a 25-year-old one 20 km out, just 3%.",
 9: "To evaluate honestly with so little data, we use cross-validated predictions: every house is predicted by a model that didn't see it. The model caught all 6 fast sellers (recall 1.0) and raised one false alarm (precision 0.86).",
 10: "With all four standardised features, plain least squares gives large weights. Ridge shrinks them — strongly at $\\alpha = 10$. Notice that size and bedrooms (which move together) share the credit more evenly once regularised.",
 11: "A new 100 m², 5-year-old house 6 km from the centre: after scaling, its 3 nearest neighbours are houses 5, 4 and 2 (88, 55 and 82 lakh), so kNN predicts their average, 75 lakh.",
 12: "Naive Bayes on the listing text alone: words like \"new\" and \"metro\" push towards selling fast, \"old\" and \"renovation\" away. \"New flat near metro\" scores 0.96; \"old villa needs renovation\" just 0.02; \"quiet flat with garden\" is mixed (0.59).",
 13: "A depth-2 tree finds a simple rule: houses within 11.5 km and no older than 16 years sold fast. It fits all 12 training houses perfectly — which, with this little data, is more likely luck than proof (Lesson 5).",
 14: "With 12 houses, leave-one-out accuracy is very noisy: a single tree scores 0.50, a random forest 0.58. The forest spreads importance across age, distance and size rather than relying on one split.",
 15: "A linear SVM on distance and age separates fast and slow sellers. Only 5 houses — the support vectors — define the boundary; the others could be removed without changing it.",
 16: "k-means with $k = 3$ on size, distance and price, without seeing the area labels, finds: small central-ish homes (cluster 0), big pricey ones (cluster 1) and far, cheap ones (cluster 2). Cluster 2 is exactly the Outskirts houses 9 and 10 — the algorithm rediscovered geography from the numbers.",
 17: "PCA on the four standardised features: the first component captures 61.5% of the variance. Its weights are all positive — it measures \"big, far and older\" all at once — and four components can be squeezed into two while keeping about 82.5% of the information.",
 18: "A tiny neural network (one hidden layer of 4 neurons) learns to predict fast sales from size, distance and age, getting every house right even in leave-one-out evaluation. On such small, simple data, though, the decision tree's rule works just as well and is far easier to explain.",
 19: "Deep learning would use the richer data we've ignored: a CNN could learn from photos of each house, and a Transformer could read the listings. Here's what a Transformer would see — a sequence of tokens; with 12 short listings the vocabulary is only 23 words, far too little to train one from scratch, so in practice you'd fine-tune a pretrained language model.",
 20: "A complete pipeline: impute a missing age, scale the numbers, one-hot encode the area, then ridge regression. Adding the area feature didn't help here (MAE 6.9 → 7.2 lakh) — distance already carries most of that information, and with 12 houses every extra column adds noise.",
}
